{"parentUuid":"915181ec-43ad-4716-9902-c2dd1200077d","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_015XFSdtBThwcPvXHYpRtCVJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01PPq3eKRPt5GNNsNBaC4iL9","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app/e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01XgaAGTCHzfLE94maFHzKqF.txt","offset":1,"limit":300}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":709,"cache_read_input_tokens":33493,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":709,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":1}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXt9zrUPRrV1y4WVVddYF","type":"assistant","uuid":"c4caeb2a-bd46-47f1-ad64-1ab7a825a9db","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:04.769Z"}
{"parentUuid":"c4caeb2a-bd46-47f1-ad64-1ab7a825a9db","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01PPq3eKRPt5GNNsNBaC4iL9","type":"tool_result","content":"     1→Pages: 24\n     2→--- PAGE 1 ---\n     3→By Norman R. Cox M.S.M.E. \n     4→\n     5→--- PAGE 2 ---\n     6→c\n     7→L \n     8→\n     9→--- PAGE 3 ---\n    10→TABLE OF CONTENTS \n    11→SECTION I . \n    12→TECHNOLOGY \n    13→PAGE \n    14→How Do You Achieve High Quality Solder Joints? ....................................................................................... \n    15→2 \n    16→What is the Importance of the Profile? ........................................................................................................... \n    17→3 \n    18→How Many Heating Zones are Required? ...................................................................................................... \n    19→4 \n    20→What is the Profile Process Band? ................................................................................................................. \n    21→5 \n    22→What are the Causes of Non-Uniform Heating? ............................................................................................ \n    23→6 \n    24→What are the Modes of Heating? .................................................................................................................... \n    25→7 \n    26→How Does Infrared Heating Work? ................................................................................................................ \n    27→8 \n    28→How Does Convection Heating Work? .......................................................................................................... \n    29→9 \n    30→What are the Benefits of IR? ... Convection? ................................................................................................ \n    31→10 \n    32→What are the Benefits of a Nitrogen Atmosphere? ........................................................................................ \n    33→11 \n    34→SECTION I1 . \n    35→EQUIPMENT SELECTION \n    36→What are the Steps in the Selection Process? ................................................................................................ \n    37→12 \n    38→How Can You Evaluate Costs? ..................................................................................................................... \n    39→13 \n    40→Selection Criteria Chart ................................................................................................................................. \n    41→14 \n    42→SECTION I11 . \n    43→RESEARCH. INC . EQUIPMENT TECHNOLOGY \n    44→Convection Oven Thermal Control ............................................................................................................... \n    45→15 \n    46→Convection Oven with Nitrogen Atmosphere ............................................................................................... \n    47→16 \n    48→IR/Convection Oven Closed Loop Control ................................................................................................... \n    49→17 \n    50→References ..................................................................................................................................................... \n    51→19 \n    52→Product Matrix .................................................................................................................. \n    53→; ............................ \n    54→20 \n    55→IR/Convection Oven with Nitrogen Atmosphere .......................................................................................... \n    56→18 \n    57→Edge Conveyor .............................................................................................................................................. \n    58→19 \n    59→.\n    60→.\n    61→.\n    62→.\n    63→Printed in the U.S.A. 0 Research. Inc., 1992 \n    64→1 \n    65→\n    66→--- PAGE 4 ---\n    67→IN \n    68→I - TECHNOLOGY \n    69→~ \n    70→HOW DO YOU ACHIEVE HIGH QUALITY SOLDER JOINTS? \n    71→The process of producing high quality solder joints is \n    72→similar to the process of learning how to ride a bicycle. \n    73→If you do not understand the fundamentals, it is virtually \n    74→impossible. However, once you understand and imple- \n    75→ment the basics, the results will follow. The goal of all \n    76→SMT process engineers is to repeatedly produce high \n    77→quality solder joints that have fine grain structure and \n    78→excellent wetting characteristics, as shown in the figure \n    79→below: \n    80→SURFACES MUST BE \n    81→CLEAN AND SOLDERABLE \n    82→II \n    83→MUST BE \n    84→1 \n    85→SMALL \n    86→CONTROL VARIABLES \n    87→In order to produce high quality solderjoints, all process \n    88→variables must be in control. The five general process \n    89→variables (l) that need to be in control include the mate- \n    90→rials, machines, manpower, methods, and the environ- \n    91→ment. If the process variables are not in control, there is \n    92→a very good chance that soldering defects will exist. \n    93→Let’s take a closer look at each. \n    94→MATERIALS \n    95→The materials must meet functional specifications so \n    96→that the product will perform to specification. Of equal \n    97→importance is the solderability of the assembly. The \n    98→solder paste/flux system must be compatible with the \n    99→base materials of the parts to be soldered. The solder \n   100→paste and parts should be as fresh as possible to enhance \n   101→solderability. \n   102→MACHINES \n   103→The machines used in the process must be able to perform \n   104→to their specifications. The machines should operate \n   105→within the range for which they are designed. Pushing a \n   106→machine beyond its operating limits will ultimately cause \n   107→a problem. The machines must be reliable and service- \n   108→able. \n   109→MANPOWER \n   110→The manpower must be properly trained. All machines in \n   111→the line require knowledgeable operators and mainte- \n   112→nance personnel. A machine is only as good as the person \n   113→operating it. \n   114→METHODS \n   115→The methods and procedures must be defined and strictly \n   116→adhered to. Inventory procedures should be implemented \n   117→to ensure that components and boards do not stay on the \n   118→shelf too long. Problems should be fully documented \n   119→including all data that pertains to it. \n   120→ENVIRONMENT \n   121→Finally, the processing environment must be controlled. \n   122→This includes both the environment inside and outside of \n   123→the machines. For example, if the humidity in the solder- \n   124→ing area is too high, this can cause the solder paste to \n   125→absorb moisture, whichmay cause problems duringreflow . \n   126→If the temperature in the room is not controlled, the \n   127→thermal profile in the reflow oven may be affected, which \n   128→again may cause a defect. \n   129→The purpose of this text is to address one aspect of high \n   130→quality SMT soldering, the reflow process. \n   131→Producing high quality solder joints \n   132→requires that five general process \n   133→variables need to be in control; the \n   134→materials, machines, manpower, \n   135→methods, and the environment. \n   136→2 \n   137→Section I - Technology \n   138→\n   139→--- PAGE 5 ---\n   140→WHAT IS THE IMPORTANCE OF THE PROFILE? \n   141→I \n   142→I \n   143→I \n   144→I \n   145→I \n   146→I \n   147→1 ;  \n   148→I \n   149→:I \n   150→I \n   151→I \n   152→I \n   153→I \n   154→In order to fully understand Surface Mount Technology \n   155→(SMT) reflow, one needs to have an understanding of \n   156→what is being accomplished. One can take too simplistic \n   157→a viewpoint in that all you need to do is to heat solder \n   158→to a temperature greater than its melting point. One can \n   159→also take too complicated a viewpoint in believing that a \n   160→certain wavelength of infrared (IR) is required, or that a \n   161→certain percentage of convection is required. Perhaps the \n   162→most astute comment made on surface mount reflow was \n   163→by an individual whom had just completed an evaluation \n   164→of vapor phase, panel IR, and lamp IR equipment nearly \n   165→five years ago. After coming up with virtually identical \n   166→soldering defect results on all three types of soldering \n   167→equipment, the following comment was made: “If you \n   168→could hit the correct reflow profile using buckets of hot \n   169→water, you would accomplish high quality, low defect \n   170→solder joints.” Although the above comment was made \n   171→in jest (hot water is not quite hot enough to reflow solder), \n   172→there is considerable truth to what was said. \n   173→I \n   174→REFLOW PROFILE REQUIREMENT \n   175→High quality, low defect soldering requires identifying \n   176→the optimum temperature profile for reflowing the solder \n   177→paste, and repeating over and over. Every solder joint on \n   178→every board, needs to be heated similarily if the desired \n   179→soldering results are to be accomplished. It does not \n   180→matter how you apply the heat to the solder joint. What \n   181→does matter is that the heat is applied to the solder joint \n   182→in a controlled manner. The heating and cooling rise \n   183→rates must be compatible with the solder paste and \n   184→components. The amount of time that the assembly is \n   185→exposed to certain temperatures must be defined and \n   186→maintained. In other words, the solder reflow profile \n   187→must first be defined and then maintained. \n   188→280- \n   189→260- \n   190→240- \n   191→T 220- \n   192→High quality, low defect soldering \n   193→requires identifying the optimum \n   194→temperature profile for reflowing \n   195→the solder paste, and repeating it \n   196→over and over. \n   197→I WETTING ; \n   198→‘ \n   199→TIME \n   200→, \n   201→30-60 .’ \n   202→: \n   203→SEC \n   204→~ \n   205→TYPICAL PROFILE \n   206→A typical profile consists of a preheat, dryout, and reflow \n   207→section (zone) where different heating rates are used. In \n   208→the preheat section, the goal is to fully preheat the SMT \n   209→assembly to temperatures generally between 100°C to \n   210→150°C. The most critical parameter in the preheat section \n   211→is to minimize the rate of rise to less than 2”C/second. \n   212→The main concern is minimizing thermal shock on the \n   213→components of the assembly. Multilayer ceramic chip \n   214→capacitors can be vulnerable to cracking if heated too \n   215→fast. Solder paste can spatter also, if heated too fast. \n   216→The second heating section, referred to as the dryout, \n   217→soak, or preflow zone, is used primarily to ensure that \n   218→the solder paste is fully dried before hitting reflow \n   219→temperatures. This zone also acts as a flux activation \n   220→zone for some solder pastes. Solder reflow is accom- \n   221→plished in the reflow zone, where the solder paste is \n   222→elevated to a temperature greater than the melting point \n   223→of the solder. For 63/37 Eutectic solder, the melting \n   224→temperature is 183°C. This temperature must be ex- \n   225→ceeded by approximately 20°C to ensure quality reflow. \n   226→The amount of time the solder joint is above the melting \n   227→point is referred to as the “wetting time”. The wetting \n   228→time is normally 30 to 60 seconds for most pastes. If the \n   229→wetting time is excessive, intermetallics may occur in \n   230→the joint, which result in brittle solder joints. In sum- \n   231→mary, define the profile, and hit it. \n   232→Section I - Technology \n   233→3 \n   234→\n   235→--- PAGE 6 ---\n   236→HOW MANY HEATING ZONES ARE REQUIRED? \n   237→This question frequently arrives and can be a source of \n   238→confusion. One theory is that if ten zones are good, then \n   239→twenty zones are better. While in theory this may be true, \n   240→in practicality it may not be true. While it improves the \n   241→flexibility of oven, it also adds to the complexity and \n   242→makes oven set-up more difficult. \n   243→Let’s review this question from the standpoint of how \n   244→many zones are really needed. Reflowing solder paste \n   245→typically requires two or three different heating rates to \n   246→accomplish the process. In the figure below, there are \n   247→three “typical” profile bands for RMA, water soluble, \n   248→and no-clean solder pastes. These profiles are rather \n   249→generic, and one should consult with their solder paste \n   250→vendor to determine the desired profile. All of the pastes \n   251→in the figure could be successfully reflowed using \n   252→three zones. \n   253→reflow is that if you can control your top and bottom \n   254→heaters separately, then double sided reflow can be \n   255→accomplished (without reflowing side A twice). This \n   256→will not work with most SMT reflow processes, as most \n   257→products are too thermally thin. Independent topbottom \n   258→heating control does help in machines with dual convey- \n   259→ors that are located at different heights in the chamber. \n   260→Product warpage may also be minimized through proper \n   261→control. The bottom line is that most machines have this \n   262→feature, but most often the top and bottom heaters will be \n   263→set at the same value. \n   264→Edge heating zones become significant when edge con- \n   265→veyors are used. Edge conveyors act as heat sinks on the \n   266→edge of the product. Two zones of edge heat (one front, \n   267→one back) will assist in compensating for edge con- \n   268→veyors. If mesh conveyors are used, these zones are \n   269→unnecessary unless the product width is quite wide. \n   270→A second issue is the question of independent top and \n   271→bottom heat control. A common conception in SMT \n   272→200 c \n   273→I \n   274→PREHEAT1 \n   275→I \n   276→PREHEAT2 \n   277→I REFLOW \n   278→I \n   279→I \n   280→I \n   281→I \n   282→I \n   283→I \n   284→I \n   285→I \n   286→I \n   287→I \n   288→I \n   289→COOLING I \n   290→TIME \n   291→4 \n   292→Section I - Technology \n   293→\n   294→--- PAGE 7 ---\n   295→WHAT IS THE PROFILE PROCESS BAND? \n   296→In reality the profile is not a line, but rather a band or \n   297→process window.(*) The size of the band is defined by the \n   298→range of temperature deviation that can occur during \n   299→reflow and yield high quality results. This is indicated \n   300→by the crosshatched area shown in the figure. In the \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"0eb3ff24-0a08-417e-bf3b-1e032c34f1ff","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:04.938Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"c4caeb2a-bd46-47f1-ad64-1ab7a825a9db"}
