{"parentUuid":"8c64361c-4209-4eba-97d1-ec525d8226b4","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01Cspm3QmG1myCavH4H54MEW","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_017zSL9rY13iFQQAqS6VV1os","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app/e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01XgaAGTCHzfLE94maFHzKqF.txt","offset":300,"limit":300}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4063,"cache_read_input_tokens":34202,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4063,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtA17W2JGPGiCtWEiLR9","type":"assistant","uuid":"14d628c2-9ab1-4497-a37e-cbd6c5ac2621","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:10.345Z"}
{"parentUuid":"14d628c2-9ab1-4497-a37e-cbd6c5ac2621","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_017zSL9rY13iFQQAqS6VV1os","type":"tool_result","content":"   300→by the crosshatched area shown in the figure. In the \n   301→example, the profile band size is indicated as 25°C. \n   302→The actual size of the band will vary depending upon \n   303→solder paste, component type, and circuit board material. \n   304→SOLDER SPATTER \n   305→AND SOLDER BALLS \n   306→CAUSED BY INSUF- \n   307→FICIENT DRYING. \n   308→BURNT BOARDS, \n   309→DEWET SOLDER fi \n   310→COLD SOLDER \n   311→JOINTS, DULL \n   312→SOLDER JOINTS \n   313→(TOO COLD) \n   314→REFLOW SOLDERING DEFECTS \n   315→If the product temperature profile is not maintained \n   316→within the band, a defect will occur. Chip capacitors \n   317→may crack if the rise rate in the preheat zone is exces- \n   318→sive. Solder spatter and solder balls may occur if drying \n   319→is excessive or insufficient. If drying is excessive, the \n   320→solder paste may skin over and not allow any of the \n   321→volatiles to escape. As the temperature of the solder joint \n   322→continues to heat, the vapor pressure increases until the \n   323→solder joint virtually explodes as the gases escape rap- \n   324→idly. The result is solder spatter and solder balls. If the \n   325→preheat is insufficient, solder spatter and balls may occur \n   326→in the reflow zone when higher heating rates occur. It is \n   327→critical that the paste is dried at low controlled heating \n   328→rates. In the reflow zone, insufficient heat will result in \n   329→non-reflowed or partially reflowed solder joints. The \n   330→joints will have a dull, rough surface finish in this case. \n   331→Excessive heating in the reflow zone may result in burnt \n   332→boards, damaged components, or possibly solder \n   333→dewetting. In a dewet solder joint, the solder is reflowed \n   334→but it is pulled away from the pad or the component lead. \n   335→DEFINE THE BAND \n   336→The profile band needs to be defined for the given SMT \n   337→assembly. To define this band, one should be aware of \n   338→not only the solder paste requirements, but also any \n   339→specific requirements of components or the circuit board \n   340→material. The process band width is defined as the total \n   341→deviation in temperature that can occur and yield reliable \n   342→results. \n   343→OVEN PERFORMANCE \n   344→From a process standpoint, the reflow oven must be able \n   345→to produce consistent results within the reflow profile \n   346→band. The user should be aware of all variables that \n   347→influence the band. These variables may be defined as \n   348→product related and oven related. By far the most critical \n   349→product related non-uniformity is that created by large \n   350→mass differences on the product. Since it is easier to heat \n   351→an area that has no components as compared with an area \n   352→with large components, temperature differentials will \n   353→exist on the product. Machine related non-uniformities \n   354→such as repeatability under product load and edge-to- \n   355→center and front-to-back product heating uniformities \n   356→must also be considered. To achieve high quality, low \n   357→defect soldering results, the sum of all the non-uniformi- \n   358→ties must fall within the defined reflow profile band. \n   359→In reality the profile is not a line, but rather \n   360→a range of temperatures all solder joints \n   361→must be exposed. \n   362→The total temperature deviation from \n   363→component thermal mismatch, oven load- \n   364→ing, and oven uniformity must be within \n   365→the band. \n   366→Section I - Technology \n   367→5 \n   368→\n   369→--- PAGE 8 ---\n   370→WHAT ARE THE CAUSES OF NON-UNIFORM HEATING? \n   371→In order to fully comprehend the capability to heat within \n   372→the defined process band, one needs to better understand \n   373→the causes of heating non-uniformities. The three main \n   374→causes of non-uniform heating in the SMT reflow pro- \n   375→cess include mass differential on the product, conveyor \n   376→and heater edge effects, and product loading. \n   377→MASS DIFFERENTIAL \n   378→The amount of temperature rise of a product as it travels \n   379→through a heating zone depends upon a number of \n   380→variables. The temperature rise of an object subjected to \n   381→heat is determined by the following equation: \n   382→AT = Q x A X  t / (M x Cp) \n   383→WHERE: \n   384→AT = Product Temperature Rise (“C) \n   385→Q = Heat Absorbed (w/cmz) \n   386→A = Exposed Area (cm2) \n   387→t = Heating Dwell Time (seconds) \n   388→M = Mass of Object (kg) \n   389→Cp = Specific Heat (w-sec/kg-”C) \n   390→The mass (M) times the specific heat (Cp) is often referred \n   391→to as thermal mass. Greater thermal mass requires more \n   392→heat to achieve a given temperature rise. In the figure \n   393→shown below, the area populated with the PLCC’s has \n   394→considerably more thermal mass per unit area than the \n   395→area with the discrete components. This area will be more \n   396→difficult to heat. The non-uniformity caused by thermal \n   397→mass differential may be measured by thermocoupling a \n   398→lead on the largest and smallest device, and running a \n   399→profile. \n   400→HIGH MASS DIFFERENTIAL \n   401→J \n   402→m m m m  \n   403→m m m m  \n   404→m m m m  \n   405→m m m m  \n   406→m m m m  \n   407→m m m m  \n   408→OVEN UNIFORMITY \n   409→A second main cause of non-uniform product heating is \n   410→the oven heating pattern. This can be caused by edge \n   411→effects on the heaters (less heat at the end) or by heat \n   412→sinking of the conveyor system. The edge effect may be \n   413→caused by insufficient airflow around the edges of the \n   414→machine, or by non-uniformities that are inherent in the \n   415→heaters. The heat sinking of the conveyor is really amass \n   416→effect, similar to the one described above. The oven \n   417→uniformity may be measured by thermocoupling a bare \n   418→board over a matrix of points, and running a profile. The \n   419→matrix should include points on the board edges (front, \n   420→back, left, and right), and the center of the board. \n   421→OVEN REPEATABILITY \n   422→Repeatability refers to the oven’s capability to repeat a \n   423→given profile. Repeatability is affected by machine \n   424→loading. The figure below shows a typical product \n   425→loading condition. The “Loading Factor” is defined in \n   426→the figure. \n   427→PRODUCT FLOW INTO OVEN b \n   428→L \n   429→LOADING FACTOR = L+s \n   430→The higher the value of the loading factor, the more \n   431→difficult it is for any oven to give repeatable results. \n   432→Ovens will generally specify a maximum loading factor \n   433→(ranging from 0.5 to 0.9). The loading factor becomes an \n   434→important consideration when sizing an oven. The non- \n   435→uniformity caused by repeatability can be measured by \n   436→running a profile on a test board, then load the oven \n   437→down. Periodically, run the test board within the oven \n   438→load and compare with the unloaded profile. \n   439→The three main causes of non-uniform heating in \n   440→the SMT reflow process include mass differential \n   441→on the product, conveyor and heater edge effects, \n   442→and product loading. \n   443→6 \n   444→Section I - Technology \n   445→\n   446→--- PAGE 9 ---\n   447→WHAT ARE THE MODES OF HEATING? \n   448→There are three different heating modes involved wit \n   449→most SMT reflow processes; convection, conductior \n   450→and infrared radiation (IR). All three of these heatin \n   451→modes occur naturally in our daily lives. Perhaps th \n   452→easiest way to understand each of these heating mode \n   453→is through example. \n   454→CONVECTION \n   455→Convection heat transfer occurs when a fluid (such a \n   456→air, nitrogen, or water) passes over a solid (such as a \n   457→SMT assembly). A cool breeze on a hot sunny da \n   458→provides convective cooling. Hot air from a hair drye \n   459→provides convective heating. Convection heating c \n   460→cooling requires contact of the flow with the solid par \n   461→Only the layer of the flow that is in contact with the pa \n   462→is actually transferring heat. \n   463→2ONVECTION I - \n   464→HOT \n   465→. \n   466→AIR \n   467→\\ \n   468→FLOW - \n   469→COLD OBJECT IS \n   470→- \n   471→HEATED BY CONVECTION \n   472→FROM THE HOT AIR FLOW \n   473→CONDUCTION \n   474→Conduction heat transfer occurs when two solid masse \n   475→of different temperature are in contact with each other \n   476→A good example is when a pan is placed on an electril \n   477→burner. Most of the heat is transferred to the pan by thl \n   478→contact between the pan and the burner. Conduction alsc \n   479→occurs within the same mass if a temperature differen \n   480→tial exists within the mass. \n   481→Referring to a system as 100% convection or \n   482→100% IR is incorrect. A system may be dominated \n   483→by convection or IR, but the other heat transfer is \n   484→always present in signijicant quantities. \n   485→CONDUCTION \n   486→HEAT FLOWS FROM HOT TO COLD TO HELP \n   487→EQUALIZE TEMPERATURES \n   488→RADIATION \n   489→Infrared Radiation (IR) occurs when two bodies of dif- \n   490→ferent temperature are in sight of each other. The best \n   491→example of IR is the heating of the earth by the sun. IR \n   492→is a non-contact heat transfer. \n   493→RADIATION I \n   494→In SMT reflow applications (with the exception of vapor \n   495→phase), convection, conduction, and IR all play a role in \n   496→the heating process. Referring to a system as 100% \n   497→convection or 100% IR is incorrect. A system may be \n   498→dominated by convection or IR, but the other heat trans- \n   499→fer is always present. IR dominant systems usually range \n   500→in the convection/IR ratio of 60/40 to 40/60. Convection \n   501→dominant systems have ratios in the range of 70/30 to \n   502→80/20. Conduction heat transfer occurs more within the \n   503→product during the heating process. Heat is transferred \n   504→through the product to help minimize hot or cold spots. \n   505→Section I - Technology \n   506→7 \n   507→\n   508→--- PAGE 10 ---\n   509→HOW DOES INFRARED HEATING WORK? \n   510→95.38 \n   511→Infrared (IR) heat transfer occurs when two objects at \n   512→different temperatures are in sight of each other. The \n   513→heat is transferred by electromagnetic waves of 0.78- \n   514→1000 micron wavelengths. All objects emit some level \n   515→of infrared. The quantity of infrared energy emitted and \n   516→the wavelength of the emission are both dependent upon \n   517→the absolute temperature of the object. As the source \n   518→temperature increases, the heat transfer output increases \n   519→exponentially to the fourth power. Increasing the source \n   520→temperature results in shorter wavelengths. Decreasing \n   521→the source temperature results in longer wavelengths. In \n   522→SMT reflow the amount of heat transfer is important, so \n   523→as not to exceed the 2OC/sec rate. When operating at \n   524→these low heating rates all types of radiant emitters \n   525→(lamps, panels, tubes) operate in the medium to long \n   526→wavelength ranges. \n   527→1\n   528→1\n   529→1\n   530→1\n   531→1\n   532→1\n   533→l\n   534→I\n   535→ \n   536→INFRARED HEATING EQUATION \n   537→In order to understand what parameters are important in \n   538→infrared heating, one can consider the general equation \n   539→for heat transfer between the heat source and the object \n   540→being heated. Again, the purpose is not to memorize the \n   541→equation, but rather to point out the significance of what \n   542→is involved. The general equation for heat transfer is as \n   543→follows: \n   544→Q = V x Es x At x K x (Ts4 - Tt4) \n   545→WHERE: \n   546→Q = Infrared heat transfer (w/cm2) \n   547→V = Geometric view factor (0-1) \n   548→Es = Emissivity factor of the source (0-1) \n   549→At = Absorptivity factor of the target (0-1) \n   550→K = Stefan-Boltzmann Constant \n   551→Ts = Source temperature (OK) \n   552→Tt = Target temperature (OK) \n   553→(5.67 x \n   554→w/cm2/”K4) \n   555→Absorption characteristics of solder \n   556→paste and circuit board material makes \n   557→IR an efficient means of heating. \n   558→The geometric view factor “V” is the fraction of energy \n   559→that leaves the source that hits the target. In SMTreflow, \n   560→the oven chamber designs yield very high view factors \n   561→in the range of 0.90 to 0.95. An important aspect of the \n   562→view factor comes in product design. If two very large \n   563→components are in close proximity to each other, the \n   564→view factor to a solder joint between them is decreased, \n   565→which makes it more difficult. Refer to figure. \n   566→f \n   567→IRSOURCE \n   568→THIS AREA IS SHADED BY / \n   569→ADJACENT COMPONENTS \n   570→RESULTING IN A LOWER VIEW \n   571→FACTOR \n   572→The source emissivity and the target absorptivity factors \n   573→are in the range on 0.90 to 0.95 for most SMT applica- \n   574→tions. Solder paste is an excellent absorber of infrared \n   575→energy. Shiny gold components may be difficult to heat \n   576→as they tend to be reflective. Most often, however, the \n   577→board material, solder paste, and the components all \n   578→absorb quite well. The figure below shows the spectral \n   579→absorptivity for an RMA solder paste.(3) \n   580→I \n   581→loooo t \n   582→! 97.39 I4 I \n   583→96’70 \n   584→96.04 I! \n   585→Control of infrared is generally done by controlling the \n   586→source temperature. Providing that the overall source \n   587→emission can be regulated, IR can provide high levels of \n   588→repeatability. \n   589→8 \n   590→Section I - Technology \n   591→\n   592→--- PAGE 11 ---\n   593→HOW DOES CONVECTION HEATING WORK? \n   594→Convection heat transfer occurs when a fluid at a given \n   595→temperature contacts a solid mass at a different tempera- \n   596→ture. If the fluid is hotter than the mass, the mass will be \n   597→heated. If the fluid is cooler than the mass, the mass will \n   598→be cooled. Perhaps the easiest way to understand convec- \n   599→tion is to look at the convection equation and note what \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"5023cf7c-b28c-4db1-999c-609780347037","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:10.369Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"14d628c2-9ab1-4497-a37e-cbd6c5ac2621"}
{"parentUuid":"5023cf7c-b28c-4db1-999c-609780347037","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","type":"progress","data":{"type":"hook_progress","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","hookName":"PostToolUse:Read","command":"callback"},"parentToolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_017zSL9rY13iFQQAqS6VV1os","toolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_017zSL9rY13iFQQAqS6VV1os","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:10.369Z","uuid":"98476966-9c25-435e-8b6a-942e4e902329"}
{"parentUuid":"98476966-9c25-435e-8b6a-942e4e902329","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01GapDtkwMYA8ezfERkqVohe","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01ETXVVzhy7gN7TVH8wTgnrP","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app/e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01XgaAGTCHzfLE94maFHzKqF.txt","offset":600,"limit":300}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4510,"cache_read_input_tokens":38265,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4510,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtA1Vwe92aBQPSz8Wjn4","type":"assistant","uuid":"41ac92d5-441d-4c60-b28d-7d7319ad4d4a","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:15.117Z"}
{"parentUuid":"41ac92d5-441d-4c60-b28d-7d7319ad4d4a","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01ETXVVzhy7gN7TVH8wTgnrP","type":"tool_result","content":"   600→is important and what is not important. \n   601→CONVECTION EQUATION \n   602→The following is the general equation for convection \n   603→heating or cooling: \n   604→Q = H x A T  \n   605→WHERE: \n   606→Q = Heat absorbed per unit area (w/cm2) \n   607→H = Convective film coefficient \n   608→AT = Temperature difference between \n   609→(W/cm2-”C) \n   610→fluid and object (“C) \n   611→The amount of heat transfer “Q’ can be modified by \n   612→either changing the convective coefficient “H’ or the \n   613→temperature difference “AT”. Increasing the source \n   614→temperature will increase the temperature difference, \n   615→and thus the amount of heat transfer “Q’ will increase \n   616→proportionally. \n   617→The convective coefficient “H’ can have a significant \n   618→impact on the heat transfer “Q”. The value of “H” is \n   619→related to a number of variables, including velocity of \n   620→the flow and angle of attack of the flow. \n   621→Increasing the flow velocity will increase the value of \n   622→“H”. For example, when one is outside in the wind, a \n   623→low velocity cool breeze on a warm day will feel quite \n   624→nice. However, a strong wind on that same day may feel \n   625→~ \n   626→~~ \n   627→~~ \n   628→Perpendicular flow convection breaks \n   629→up surface thermal boundary layers, \n   630→which enhances heat transfer. \n   631→very cold. That is because the higher velocity wind has \n   632→a higher convective coefficient, and thus removes heat \n   633→more effectively from your skin. In SMT reflow appli- \n   634→cations, increasing the velocity of the flow also improves \n   635→the amount of convection heat transfer. There are prac- \n   636→tical limits to this, as too high of velocity may cause \n   637→components to shift around. The flow direction also has \n   638→a significant impact on “Q’. Parallel flow convection \n   639→is shown below: \n   640→PARALLEL FLOW WIBOUNDARY LAYER \n   641→I FLOW DIRECTION 4 - \n   642→BOUNDARY LAYER FORMATION \n   643→CAUSES STAGNANT FLOW NEAR \n   644→BOARD, WHICH REDUCES \n   645→HEAT TRANSFER \n   646→Note that in the parallel flow diagram, there is an area \n   647→referred to as the “boundary layer”. In the boundary \n   648→layer, there is no flow motion, which causes convective \n   649→heat transfer to be very low. It is beneficial to break up \n   650→the boundary layer to allow the flow to transfer heat \n   651→freely to the object. The diagram shown below shows \n   652→perpendicular flow convection: \n   653→PERPENDICULAR FLOW \n   654→FLOW DIRECTION \n   655→t\n   656→t\n   657→t\n   658→ \n   659→NO BOUNDARY \n   660→LAYER \n   661→NO BOUNDARY LAYER FORMATION \n   662→ENHANCES CONVECTIVE HEAT \n   663→TRANSFER \n   664→In the diagram showing perpendicular flow, note that \n   665→there is no boundary layer. This is the reason that \n   666→perpendicular flow yields better convective heating or \n   667→cooling results. \n   668→Section I - Technology \n   669→9 \n   670→\n   671→--- PAGE 12 ---\n   672→WHAT ARE THE BENEFITS OF IR? ... CONVECTION? \n   673→In SMT reflow applications, there is no such thing as \n   674→pure convection or pure IR. The only way to achieve a \n   675→100% convection system would be to have gas flowing \n   676→over the product with no chamber walls. Of course this \n   677→in not practical. To have a 100% IR system, the chamber \n   678→would need to be operated in a vacuum. Again, this is not \n   679→the case. However, some systems are more dominated by \n   680→convection, while others utilize IR as the primary heat \n   681→source. For the sake of simplicity this text will refer to a \n   682→convection dominated machine as a convection ma- \n   683→chine, and an IR dominate machine as an IR machine. \n   684→CONVECTION \n   685→Both convection and IR have their unique advantages \n   686→and limitations. In convective systems, typically the \n   687→temperature of the flow is close to the desired tempera- \n   688→ture of the product in a given zone. This has the \n   689→advantage in that the product temperature will never \n   690→exceed the gas flow temperature. This is useful when \n   691→heating a product that has a large difference in the mass \n   692→of the components and distribution. In this case, the \n   693→larger components will continue to heat, while the smaller \n   694→components will not overheat. Convection heat transfer \n   695→is not thermally efficient. Air (or nitrogen) has a very low \n   696→heat capacity, which means that high volumes of flow \n   697→may be required to transfer the heat. One way to improve \n   698→efficiency and reduce the amount of process gas required \n   699→CONVECTION \n   700→Less sensitive to shading \n   701→Heats more uniformly \n   702→AT (Source-target) is small \n   703→Low thermal mass of air requires \n   704→high flow rates to transfer heat \n   705→Inefficient (20 to 30%) \n   706→Will heat leads first, increasing \n   707→chance of solder wicking. \n   708→is through the use of recirculation. In a recirculation \n   709→loop, the process gas from a zone is evacuated, then \n   710→reheated and re-input into the heating chamber. The \n   711→limitation to recirculating is that high nitrogen purity \n   712→levels are not typically attainable. There is also a concern \n   713→that if the gas is recirculated excessively, it may affect \n   714→soldering quality, as the atmosphere surrounding the \n   715→product becomes contaminated with process by-prod- \n   716→ucts. Convection increases the chance of solder wicking, \n   717→as typically the lead is heated faster than the circuit board. \n   718→IR \n   719→IR systems have the distinct advantage of high heating \n   720→efficiencies and low process gas flows required to reflow \n   721→solder. IR systems are normally very repeatable, and \n   722→have the capability to transfer high quantities of heat as \n   723→needed. This becomes quite useful when reflowing very \n   724→dense SMT assemblies or very heavy boards. The \n   725→limitation with IR is that a high temperature differential \n   726→between the source and the product is required to transfer \n   727→the heat. Products with high component mass differen- \n   728→tial do not heat uniformly. They must be run quite slowly \n   729→to accomplish reflow. IR systems are successful at \n   730→reflowing products with component sizes up to and \n   731→including 84 pin PLCC’s. Large connectors and pin grid \n   732→arrays are quite difficult to reflow on an IR dominated \n   733→system. \n   734→IR \n   735→Control source temperature only \n   736→High efficiency (60-70%) \n   737→Easy to control rate of rise \n   738→More sensitive to shading \n   739→Less uniform high mass differences \n   740→AT (Source-target) is large \n   741→10 \n   742→Section I - Technology \n   743→\n   744→--- PAGE 13 ---\n   745→WHAT ARE THE BENEFITS OF A NITROGEN ATMOSPHERE? \n   746→~ \n   747→Ease of cleaning flux \n   748→residues (RMA) \n   749→Reflow soldering in a nitrogen atmosphere is becoming \n   750→a significant process consideration. The issue is really \n   751→not the ability to reflow in nitrogen, but rather the ability \n   752→to reflow in the absence of oxygen. Heating solder in \n   753→the presence of oxygen will create oxides, which are \n   754→generally non-solderable surfaces. In past days of reflow \n   755→soldering, the solder paste consisted of aggressive enough \n   756→fluxes to remove oxides during the reflow process. The \n   757→future trend is moving away from strong fluxes in the \n   758→paste. The future trends include two major process \n   759→changes that are driving the need for reflow in nitrogen \n   760→atmospheres; fine pitch soldering and no-clean fluxes. \n   761→500 ppm \n   762→FINE PITCH REFLOW SOLDERING \n   763→Fine pitch soldering presents many challenges. In order \n   764→to obtain satisfactory paste print definition, the particle \n   765→sizes of tin and lead are becoming smaller. As the \n   766→particles become smaller, the ratio of surface area to \n   767→volume of the particles increases. This can be shown by: \n   768→RATIO = (n x D*) /(1/6 x n x D3) \n   769→RATIO = 6 / D \n   770→WHERE: \n   771→D = Particle Diameter \n   772→FOR THE SAME VOLUME (OR MASS) OF SOLDER, THERE IS \n   773→GREATER SURFACE AREA WITH SMALLER PARTICLES THAN \n   774→WITH LARGER PARTICLES. \n   775→Thus, as particles sizes decrease, the ratio increases. \n   776→With more available surface area in the solder paste, \n   777→there is a greater tendency towards oxidation. Keep in \n   778→mind that oxidation is a function of the metals involved, \n   779→the temperatures of the metal, the exposed surface area, \n   780→and the amount of oxygen present. The only variable \n   781→that one can control is the amount of oxygen present. \n   782→This can only be done through proper oven design. \n   783→NO-CLEAN SOLDERING \n   784→The second technology change that is driving the need \n   785→towards nitrogen atmospheres is the greater usage of no- \n   786→clean pastes. The rapid trend away from solvent cleaning \n   787→is creating this and other needs. No-clean soldering \n   788→requires a tight control of the process. All of the compo- \n   789→nents and boards must be highly solderable. The reflow \n   790→process must not add any significant oxidation to the \n   791→process. The fluxes used are very weak, and do not have \n   792→aggressive oxide cleaning action. In addition, there is no \n   793→cleaning that would remove any solder balls from the \n   794→product. These solder balls eventually can create short \n   795→circuits on the product if they are not removed. Reflowing \n   796→in a high purity nitrogen environment reduces the possi- \n   797→bility of creating solder balls. \n   798→A good question to ask is “How pure a nitrogen environ- \n   799→ment is required?” A recent study (4) by Colin Lea of the \n   800→National Physics Laboratory in the United Kingdom was \n   801→conducted to determine the affect of nitrogen purity on \n   802→the process. The conclusions drawn in the study are \n   803→listed below: \n   804→DESCRIPTION \n   805→I \n   806→PURITY LEVEL I \n   807→Significant reduction \n   808→in board discoloration \n   809→10,000 ppm \n   810→Reduction in solder \n   811→200 ppm \n   812→balling (RMA) \n   813→Reflow of low-residue \n   814→solder paste \n   815→< 100 ppm \n   816→The paper presents a number of excellent graphs that \n   817→show the affect of nitrogen purity on parameters includ- \n   818→ing flux spread, wetting force, wetting time, solder balls, \n   819→cleaning, residue, board discoloration, and micro-struc- \n   820→ture. It is recommended reading if your process may \n   821→require the use of nitrogen. You must determine the \n   822→nitrogen requirements for your process. The benefits of \n   823→using nitrogen definitely exist, however you must deter- \n   824→mine if the additional cost of nitrogen is worthwhile for \n   825→your process. \n   826→Section I - Technology \n   827→11 \n   828→\n   829→--- PAGE 14 ---\n   830→1 SECTION II - EQUIPMENT SELECTION I \n   831→WHAT ARE THE STEPS IN THE SELECTION PROCESS? \n   832→When selecting reflow equipment, it is important to take \n   833→into consideration all aspects that may affect the process \n   834→and production. Try to avoid becoming too focussed on \n   835→one issue, such as populated uniformity, as it may not \n   836→lead you to the optimum decision. The recommended \n   837→steps in the selection process are described in the follow- \n   838→ing paragraphs. \n   839→DEFINE PROCESS BAND WIDTH \n   840→The first step is to define the acceptable process profile \n   841→band width for your SMT assemblies. Try to be as \n   842→accurate as possible when defining the band. If the band \n   843→is too restrictive, some aspect of production will be \n   844→negatively affected. If the band is too loose, a defect will \n   845→show up in the process. The band width should be \n   846→defined as a range of temperatures (\"C) that the entire \n   847→profile must fall within. Refer to page 4 for a more \n   848→detailed description. \n   849→DEFINE HEATING NON-UNIFORMITIES \n   850→The next step is to define causes of heating non-unifor- \n   851→mities that may occur in the process. These non-unifor- \n   852→mities are caused by mass differential, oven uniformity, \n   853→and oven repeatability under production loading condi- \n   854→tions. A set of tests must be conducted with the desired \n   855→product on the oven in question. The sum of the non- \n   856→uniformities (\"C) must be less than the profile band width \n   857→defined in the first step. Refer to page 5 for a more \n   858→detailed description. \n   859→SIZE THE OVEN PROPERLY \n   860→The oven must be sized to handle the production rate of \n   861→the pick-and-place equipment. The calculation is shown \n   862→below: \n   863→LINE SPEED - BOARDS PER MINUTE X LENGTH PER BOARD \n   864→(MINIMUM) - \n   865→LOAD FACTOR \n   866→Operating at a conveyor speed greater than the calculated \n   867→speed ensures that no product jams will occur on the \n   868→onload area of the reflow oven. \n   869→Try to avoid becoming too focussed on one \n   870→issue, such as populated uniformity, as it may \n   871→not lead you to the optimum decision. \n   872→In order for the oven to be properly sized, the process \n   873→speed defined below must be greater than the minimum \n   874→calculated line speed. This will ensure that bottlenecks \n   875→will not occur at the reflow oven. The process speed \n   876→should be determined through testing or by the following \n   877→equation: \n   878→OVEN CHAMBER HEATED LENGTH \n   879→PROCESS - \n   880→SPEED - \n   881→PROCESS DWELL TIME \n   882→DEFINE PROCESS GAS \n   883→If a nitrogen atmosphere is required for solderability or \n   884→no-clean processing (see page lo), one needs to consider \n   885→the purity required and the gas consumption. Be careful \n   886→how the purity is measured, such that it is not measured \n   887→at the gas inlet. \n   888→REVIEW CONVEYOR DESIGN \n   889→The conveying mechanism is perhaps the most over- \n   890→looked aspect of equipment selection. One needs to \n   891→define whether a mesh conveyor or an edge type is \n   892→required. Typically edge conveyors are used for in-line \n   893→processing or if double-sided assembly is required. The \n   894→most important issue with edge conveyors is the ability \n   895→for the conveyor to remain straight to avoid dropping \n   896→product. \n   897→RELIABILITY/SERVICEABILITY \n   898→Once a machine is placed into service, it is critical that it \n   899→remains operational. Evaluate the product design from a \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"96c425c0-eea2-458d-8e92-6965155104e8","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:15.561Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"41ac92d5-441d-4c60-b28d-7d7319ad4d4a"}
{"parentUuid":"96c425c0-eea2-458d-8e92-6965155104e8","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","type":"progress","data":{"type":"hook_progress","hookEvent":"PostToolUse","hookName":"PostToolUse:Read","command":"callback"},"parentToolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_01ETXVVzhy7gN7TVH8wTgnrP","toolUseID":"toolu_vrtx_01ETXVVzhy7gN7TVH8wTgnrP","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:15.561Z","uuid":"e9c30c16-5571-42f7-980c-180019e44817"}
{"parentUuid":"e9c30c16-5571-42f7-980c-180019e44817","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01Um23gW15sQwcfXpfZPvnt7","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_01KgVZhwmiy71s2zpLGt16xB","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app/e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01XgaAGTCHzfLE94maFHzKqF.txt","offset":900,"limit":300}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4476,"cache_read_input_tokens":42775,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4476,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtA1tpZ6DjyzryF32iBB","type":"assistant","uuid":"1dc0ac6b-030e-4c58-a5e2-1bd85c40c413","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:21.244Z"}
{"parentUuid":"1dc0ac6b-030e-4c58-a5e2-1bd85c40c413","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_01KgVZhwmiy71s2zpLGt16xB","type":"tool_result","content":"   900→structural as well as a functional standpoint. Is the oven \n   901→designed to withstand the rigors of production? Review \n   902→the product manuals, drawings, and spare parts lists. \n   903→Find out where the nearest servicing center is located. Is \n   904→training available? These are the questions that must be \n   905→asked during the selection process. \n   906→INTERFACING REQUIREMENTS \n   907→Conveyor and control interfacing requirements may also \n   908→be an important selection issue. Make sure that the edge \n   909→conveyor fixed rail justification is the same as the pick- \n   910→and-place equipment. Ask questions about networking \n   911→machines to a host and product flow control schemes if \n   912→needed. \n   913→12 \n   914→Section I1 - Equipment Selection \n   915→\n   916→--- PAGE 15 ---\n   917→HOW CAN YOU EVALUATE COSTS? \n   918→DESCRIPTION \n   919→The selection steps listed on the previous page should \n   920→all be considered when comparing equipment. When \n   921→multiple ovens pass the selection criteria listed, then a \n   922→proper cost evaluation may be used to compare ovens. \n   923→This is especially important when comparing ovens that \n   924→utilize different heating technologies, as first cost and \n   925→operating costs can be significantly different. This \n   926→section describes various costs that may be included in \n   927→the analysis. \n   928→HOT GAS \n   929→IR \n   930→EQUIPMENT COST \n   931→Often, the initial cost of the equipment is the only cost \n   932→considered when comparing equipment. It is certainly \n   933→important, although it is only a part of the total cost \n   934→picture. To make a fair comparison, all costs should be \n   935→analyzed with a common basis, such as annual cost. As \n   936→an example let’s look at amachine that costs $50,000 and \n   937→has an expected life of 5 years, with an interest rate of \n   938→10%. Assume the value of the equipment is zero at the \n   939→end of five years. The annualized cost of the equipment \n   940→is equal to the initial cost times the Capital Recover \n   941→Factor. (3 For an interest rate of 10% with a period of \n   942→5 years, the CRF value is equal to 0.2638. The annu- \n   943→alized cost is then equal to: \n   944→Annual Cost = $50,000 x 0.2638 \n   945→= $13,190 \n   946→~ \n   947→~ \n   948→GAS COST \n   949→PER 1000 CFH \n   950→OPERATING COST \n   951→The operating cost needs to include the electrical power \n   952→to operate the machine and also the cost of any process \n   953→gases (such as nitrogen). If there are other pertinent \n   954→operating costs, include them in this analysis. \n   955→$5.00 \n   956→When multiple ovens pass the selection criteria \n   957→listed, then aproper cost evaluation may be used \n   958→to compare ovens. \n   959→POWER COST \n   960→PER KW-HR \n   961→The importance of factoring in operating costs \n   962→should be noted. \n   963→$0.10 \n   964→MAINTENANCE AND SPARE PARTS COSTS \n   965→The maintenance and spare parts costs should be in- \n   966→cluded in the analysis. This may be the most difficult to \n   967→predict, but it should be included. \n   968→SUMMARY \n   969→- \n   970→An example of an annual cost analysis is shown in the \n   971→table below, comparing a convection (hot gas) machine \n   972→to an IR machine. The values used are not specific to any \n   973→vendor’s equipment or products, merely typical values \n   974→from the market. The total annual estimated costs reveal \n   975→the importance of including the operational costs in the \n   976→analysis. \n   977→- \n   978→- \n   979→CRF \n   980→(FROM ABOVE) \n   981→0.2638 \n   982→0.2638 \n   983→GAS USAGE \n   984→I 1800CFH I 1200CFH I \n   985→EQUIPMENT \n   986→$13,190 \n   987→GAS COST \n   988→$18,720 \n   989→POWER COST \n   990→$4,160 \n   991→POWERUSAGE I 20KW \n   992→I 12KW \n   993→I \n   994→$15,828 \n   995→$12,480 \n   996→$2,496 \n   997→HOURSEEAR \n   998→2080 HRS \n   999→2080 HRS \n  1000→-+-I- INITIAL COST \n  1001→$50,000 \n  1002→$60,000 \n  1003→MAINTENANCE \n  1004→TOTAL \n  1005→$ 1,000 \n  1006→$1,000 \n  1007→$37,070 \n  1008→$31,804 \n  1009→$5.00 \n  1010→$0.10 \n  1011→Section I1 - Equipment Selection \n  1012→13 \n  1013→\n  1014→--- PAGE 16 ---\n  1015→SELECTION CRITERIA CHART \n  1016→PROCESS VARIABLE \n  1017→VENDOR A \n  1018→VENDOR B \n  1019→VENDOR C \n  1020→PROCESS BAND WIDTH REQUIRED (\"C) \n  1021→H EAT1 NG NON-U N I FO RMlTl ES \n  1022→MASS DIFFERENTIAL (\"C) \n  1023→OVEN UNIFORMITY (\"C) \n  1024→I \n  1025→REPEATABILITY (\"C) \n  1026→I \n  1027→I \n  1028→1 \n  1029→MAXIMUM LOADING FACTOR \n  1030→PROCESS SPEED \n  1031→I \n  1032→PROCESS GAS TYPE \n  1033→DRYOUT ZONE PURITY (PPM) \n  1034→I PROCESS GAS FLOW \n  1035→POWER REQUIRED \n  1036→UNDER PRODUCTION LOAD (KW) \n  1037→CONVEYOR TYPE \n  1038→CONVEYOR STRAIGHTNESS \n  1039→(IF EDGE) \n  1040→CONVEYOR DIRECTION/ \n  1041→RAIL JUSTIFICATION \n  1042→GENERAL MACHINE QUALITY \n  1043→RATING (1-10) \n  1044→MANUALS AND DOCUMENTATION \n  1045→RATING (1-10) \n  1046→SERVICING LOCATION \n  1047→COMPUTER INTERFACE AVAILABLE \n  1048→ ANNUAL COST TO OPERATE \n  1049→14 \n  1050→Section I1 - Equipment Selection \n  1051→\n  1052→--- PAGE 17 ---\n  1053→! SECTION III - RESEARCH, INC. EQUIPMENT TECHNOLOGY~ \n  1054→CONVECTION OVEN THERMAL CONTROL \n  1055→Convection oven thermal control requires tight control \n  1056→of two variables; temperature and flow. In order to \n  1057→maintain constant heat flux to the product, it is critical \n  1058→that both these variables are controlled. Research, Inc.’s \n  1059→MICRI-FLO XG forced convection reflow system will \n  1060→be used as an example. The temperature and flow control \n  1061→are defined specific to this system. \n  1062→CONVECTION OVEN DESCRIPTION \n  1063→Temperature control of the system must consider both \n  1064→the gas and the surrounding wall temperature. Remem- \n  1065→ber, even in a convection dominant heating system, the \n  1066→walls radiate IR energy worth about 20%-30% of the \n  1067→total heat flux of the system. The figure below shows \n  1068→the heater cavity flow diagram for the MICRI-FLO XG. \n  1069→The reflow and dryout zones each receive input gas \n  1070→(air or nitrogen) through a series of flow meters. This \n  1071→controlled flow enters a diffusion plenum behind the \n  1072→heating plenum to distribute the flow. The flow then \n  1073→enters a second plenum, where the gas is preheated by a \n  1074→series of heaters. The heaters consist of a wound nickel- \n  1075→chromium wire encased in a stainless steel jacket. The \n  1076→GAS FLOW INPUT FROM \n  1077→AIR OR NITROGEN SOURCE \n  1078→II \n  1079→4 \n  1080→Y \n  1081→GAS FLOW INPUT FROM AIR OR NITROGEN SOURCE \n  1082→heaters are bent in a “U” shape such that easy removal \n  1083→can occur from one side of the machine. The gas then \n  1084→enters the process chamber through an array of holes. \n  1085→The product is heated from a combination of the hot gas \n  1086→and the hot chamber surroundings. A control thermo- \n  1087→couple is mounted in each zone to maintain cavity \n  1088→repeatability. A PID (Proportional - Integral - Deriva- \n  1089→tive) control loop is used to maintain the cavity at \n  1090→constant conditions. \n  1091→The flow is removed from the reflow area by two exhaust \n  1092→ducts located on each side of the reflow zone. This flow \n  1093→is pulled from the zones using a high temperature, low \n  1094→flow recirculating blower. The flow is pushed by the \n  1095→blower through the preheater plenum arrangement. The \n  1096→temperature control is accomplished in the same way as \n  1097→the dryout and reflow zone. \n  1098→The flow that heats the product (in all zones) is forced \n  1099→through the walls in an array of holes to break up the \n  1100→boundary layers on the product. The perpendicular flow \n  1101→arrangement shown on page 8 is used to optimize heat \n  1102→transfer. \n  1103→Convection oven thermal control requires tight \n  1104→control of two variables; temperature andflow. \n  1105→In order to maintain constant heat flux to the \n  1106→product, it is critical that both these variables \n  1107→are controlled. \n  1108→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \n  1109→15 \n  1110→\n  1111→--- PAGE 18 ---\n  1112→CONVECTION OVEN WITH \n  1113→Convection ovens are a greater challenge to inert for \n  1114→several reasons. First, the amount of flow that would be \n  1115→required to transfer 80% of the energy by convection is \n  1116→extremely high, and cost prohibitive. In order to mini- \n  1117→mize this, recirculation ducts are added, which result in \n  1118→the degradation of the purity of the source flow entering \n  1119→a given zone. Higher velocities and turbulent flow are \n  1120→also required, which can result in a greater opportunity \n  1121→for oxygen to enter the system. Third, the pressure in the \n  1122→heater cavity is positive in some areas, and negative in \n  1123→others. Balancing this flow becomes adifficult task. The \n  1124→job of inerting a convection oven is not impossible. \n  1125→However, it should be noted that purity levels and flow \n  1126→rates are less optimum than with an IR based machine. \n  1127→CHAMBER DESCRIPTION \n  1128→Many of the same principles apply for inerting when \n  1129→comparing a convection oven to an IR oven. The MZCRZ- \n  1130→FLO XG convection oven shown below utilizes a very \n  1131→similar scheme to the 4470XN IR oven. The gas is \n  1132→brought into each zone very uniformly using the propri- \n  1133→VITROGEN ATMOSPHERE \n  1134→etary gas plenum distribution system. The pressure in the \n  1135→entry and exit purge sections are maintained at sufficient \n  1136→levels to minimize oxygen infiltration. Flow meter \n  1137→controlled venturi exhausts are used to balance the flow \n  1138→in the system and to remove process volatiles. \n  1139→In order to maintain the best purity levels in the zones \n  1140→where the product temperature is greater than 150°C, \n  1141→pure nitrogen is delivered to the reflow and dryout \n  1142→zone. This is a key point, as this ensures that the product \n  1143→experiences a nearly pure environment at its most criti- \n  1144→cal stages (dryout and reflow). The recirculated gas \n  1145→from these zones is reheated and used in the preheat zone. \n  1146→Here, purity is not as critical, so the use of recirculated \n  1147→gas is not detrimental to the process. \n  1148→Purity levels in the dryout and reflow zone can be \n  1149→maintained at 35 PPM over incoming purity levels at \n  1150→flow rates of 1800 cubic feet per hour (5 1 cubic meters \n  1151→per hour). \n  1152→GAS FLOW \n  1153→INPUT FROM \n  1154→N2 OR AIR \n  1155→SOURCE \n  1156→GAS FLOW \n  1157→INPUT FROM \n  1158→N2 OR AIR \n  1159→SOURCE \n  1160→16 \n  1161→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \n  1162→\n  1163→--- PAGE 19 ---\n  1164→IR/CONVECTION OVEN CLOSED LOOP CONTROL \n  1165→One of the key oven performance issues is the ability to \n  1166→repeat a product temperature profile. Repeatability is \n  1167→influenced most by product loading. Heavy or non- \n  1168→variable loading can result in the oven incorrectly com- \n  1169→pensating, resulting in a loss of repeatability. High levels \n  1170→of repeatability can be achieved only if all the compo- \n  1171→nents of heat transfer are maintained constant. \n  1172→LAMP DEFINITION \n  1173→The closed loop heat flux control method used in a \n  1174→Research, Inc. lamp based oven will be used as an \n  1175→example of one type of control. The primary heat source \n  1176→in this system is the tungsten filament (T3) lamp. The T3 \n  1177→lamp consists of a tungsten filament encased in a sealed \n  1178→quartz envelope. The envelope is filled with an inert gas \n  1179→to keep the filament from oxidizing and failing. The \n  1180→lamps are rated for 5000 hours when operated at 100% \n  1181→capacity. In SMT applications, the lamp power never \n  1182→exceeds 20% capacity, resulting in lamp life in excess of \n  1183→20,000 hours. At typical operating setpoints of 5-15%, \n  1184→the heat transfer to the product is controlled such that \n  1185→heating rates are under 2”C/second, and the IR emission \n  1186→is in the medium to long wavelength range. \n  1187→The greatest advantage that T3 lamps have is their rapid \n  1188→response capability. Within 1 to 2 seconds after a voltage \n  1189→change occurs, the lamp is at its new temperature equi- \n  1190→librium. This rapid response is ideal for closed loop \n  1191→control, as energy can be replaced in the heating chamber \n  1192→instantly after its removal. \n  1193→CONTROL DEFINITION \n  1194→In order for the product profile to be repeatable, the total \n  1195→heat flux to the product must be held constant. The total \n  1196→heat flux to the product is the sum of IR from the lamp and \n  1197→wall plus the convective component. Refer to the figure. \n  1198→Within 1 to 2 seconds after a voltage change \n  1199→occurs, the lamp is at its new temperature \n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"445a7f52-6530-44e0-96e2-84fa501b2e8f","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:21.278Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"1dc0ac6b-030e-4c58-a5e2-1bd85c40c413"}
