{"parentUuid":"b7761f0f-383a-44b6-947d-b36bcdd7a2f2","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_vrtx_01MeDm7oCrpbyD1pXjCpWMHQ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_vrtx_013KEanCGFMTaqvuon76Youb","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/logs/agent/sessions/projects/-app/e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09/tool-results/toolu_vrtx_01XgaAGTCHzfLE94maFHzKqF.txt","offset":1200,"limit":400}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4505,"cache_read_input_tokens":47251,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":4505,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":18}},"requestId":"req_vrtx_011CXtA2KapsaWQsUHQMPxVP","type":"assistant","uuid":"5eae4a8a-a94e-42a5-888a-324539bc96cb","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:27.196Z"}
{"parentUuid":"5eae4a8a-a94e-42a5-888a-324539bc96cb","isSidechain":true,"userType":"external","cwd":"/app","sessionId":"e7416bc3-d779-440d-912b-4c10d3ea2c09","version":"2.1.19","gitBranch":"","agentId":"a1adf20","slug":"abstract-snuggling-corbato","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_vrtx_013KEanCGFMTaqvuon76Youb","type":"tool_result","content":"  1200→equilibrium. This rapid response is ideal for \n  1201→closed loop control, as energy can be replaced \n  1202→in the heating chamber instantly after its \n  1203→removal. \n  1204→RAPID-RESPONSE (EXPOSED-TIP) \n  1205→THERMOCOUPLE \n  1206→PRODUCT ”‘7 \\ R E F L F  \n  1207→JON. \n  1208→$ RE-EMITTED \n  1209→IR \n  1210→VECTION \n  1211→1 \n  1212→FLOW -- \n  1213→\\P \n  1214→PRODUCT \n  1215→Accurate contol is not achieved by maintaining only one \n  1216→of the components constant, but rather the sum of all the \n  1217→thermal energies involved. The lamp IR component is \n  1218→regulated by the SCRpower controlled system. The wall \n  1219→and convective components are collectively monitored \n  1220→by the bulk chamber temperature measured with a ther- \n  1221→mocouple probe. There are two reference setpoints; \n  1222→percent voltage for the lamp, and the bulk chamber \n  1223→temperature. The bulk chamber setpoint drives the \n  1224→equation. If the chamber is at the correct temperature, \n  1225→then the lamp setpoints will be at their setpoints. If the \n  1226→chamber is too hot, then the lamps will decrease in \n  1227→intensity. If the chamber is too cool, the lamps will \n  1228→operate at a level greater than their setpoint. The lamp \n  1229→power is modified only enough to compensate for the \n  1230→bulk chamber heat transfer deviation. In doing so, the \n  1231→total heat flux to the product is maintained. The correct \n  1232→compensation will occur providing the bulk chamber \n  1233→temperature is within k 25°C. Product temperature \n  1234→repeatability o f f  2.5”C will occur if the bulk chamber \n  1235→is within this defined band. \n  1236→The ability to operate within this wide band has two \n  1237→significant advantages. The first is that the oven may \n  1238→be loaded down to levels up to 80% load. The second is \n  1239→that profile changeover times are reduced significantly. \n  1240→Loading may begin when all oven temperatures are \n  1241→within f 25°C of setpoint. This requires far less time to \n  1242→achieve the “ready” state than if the “ready” process \n  1243→window was f 3”C, which is often the case inreflow oven \n  1244→operation. Warm-up times of less than 15 minutes occur. \n  1245→Profile changeover times are 3 to 5 minutes for profile \n  1246→increases of under 40”C, and under 10 minutes for profile \n  1247→decreases of under 40°C. Even in the worst case of going \n  1248→from a reflow profile to an adhesive cure profile, the \n  1249→changeover time is under 15 minutes. \n  1250→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \n  1251→17 \n  1252→\n  1253→--- PAGE 20 ---\n  1254→IR/CONVECTION OVEN WITH NITROGEN ATMOSPHERE \n  1255→PREHEAT TOP (IR1) \n  1256→Infrared ovens do not require high quantities of flow to \n  1257→accomplish reflow. The predominant heat transfer oc- \n  1258→curs with IR, which does not require any flow to transfer \n  1259→the heat. The flow in the oven is used to provide some of \n  1260→the benefits of convection, which includes improving \n  1261→populated board uniformity and volatile removal. The \n  1262→amount of flow in the oven is more related to the quantity \n  1263→required to accomplish a high purity nitrogen environ- \n  1264→ment, which is considerably less than in the case of a \n  1265→convection oven. \n  1266→REFLOW \n  1267→TOP (IR5) \n  1268→DRY TOP (IR3) \n  1269→PREFLOW TOP (IR4) \n  1270→NITROGEN OVEN DESCRIPTION \n  1271→The 4470XN from Research, Inc. is an example of an IR \n  1272→dominant oven that provides a high purity nitrogen \n  1273→environment at low flow levels. \n  1274→The system uses a \n  1275→proprietary gas flow distribution method through the \n  1276→chamber walls to uniformly blanket the chamber with the \n  1277→process nitrogen. A slight positive pressure inside the \n  1278→chamber helps minimize external room effects and helps \n  1279→keep oxygen out of the chamber. Entry and exit purge \n  1280→sections are present to provide a nitrogen curtain at the \n  1281→chamber entrance and exit, which also assists in keeping \n  1282→oxygen out of the chamber. Flow meter controlled \n  1283→venturi exhausts are used to balance the flow in the \n  1284→system and to remove process volatiles. \n  1285→PREHEAT BOTTOM (IR2) \n  1286→The gas system is non-recirculatory, in that the gas is \n  1287→used once and then expended. This has the advantage \n  1288→that process contaminants are not recirculated which \n  1289→helps maintain high nitrogen purity levels. The heat \n  1290→chamber is accessible by unfastening six clamps and \n  1291→elevating the top heater vertically through the use of a \n  1292→crank mechanism. Purity levels of 20 PPM (over in- \n  1293→coming purity level) are attainable in all heating zones. \n  1294→Flow rates are in the 1200 to 1600 cubic feet per hour \n  1295→(34 to 45 cubic meters per hour) range. Machines in the \n  1296→field are experiencing total purity levels of 10-15 PPM \n  1297→(including incoming) at flow rates of 1300 cubic feet per \n  1298→hour (37 cubic meters per hour). These purity levels are \n  1299→measured with the machine fully loaded with product. \n  1300→REFLOW \n  1301→BOTTOM (1R6) \n  1302→DRY BOTTOM (IR3) \n  1303→PREFLOW BOTTOM (IR4) \n  1304→~~~ \n  1305→~ \n  1306→The amount of flow in the oven is more \n  1307→related to the quantity required to accom- \n  1308→plish a high purity nitrogen environment, \n  1309→which is considerably less than in the case \n  1310→of a convection oven. \n  1311→NOTE: VENTURI EXHAUSTS ARE ACTIVATED BY COMPRESSED AIR, \n  1312→ALL OTHERS MAY BE NITROGEN OR COMPRESSED AIR. \n  1313→18 \n  1314→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \n  1315→\n  1316→--- PAGE 21 ---\n  1317→EDGE CONVEYOR \n  1318→Proper design of the edge conveying system is critical, \n  1319→as typically only 0.17 inches (4 mm) is in contact with \n  1320→the bottom side edges of the product. If the conveyor \n  1321→has lateral warpage of greater than this amount, the \n  1322→product will fall into the oven. Thus, edge conveyor \n  1323→straightness is a key issue in SMT processing. \n  1324→As the conveyor rails heat during the machine warm-up \n  1325→period, they will grow and tend to warp. The patented \n  1326→system from Research, Inc. shown below tensions the \n  1327→rails, which pulls the rail assembly straight. The lever \n  1328→(outlined in dark) is attached to the rail. The bottom side \n  1329→of the lever is attached to an adjustable spring mechanism \n  1330→that runs from end to end in the machine. Approximately \n  1331→1500 lbs (681 kg) of tensioning force is applied to each \n  1332→rail to maintain straightness. The machine frame is made \n  1333→of welded structural tubing in order to maintain its \n  1334→structural integrity. \n  1335→The use of the tensioning system allows the mass of the \n  1336→rails to be maintained at a reasonably low value, which \n  1337→minimizes the thermal affect of the rail on the product. \n  1338→As the conveyo’r rails heat during the ma- \n  1339→chine warm-up period, they will grow and \n  1340→tend to warp. The patented system from \n  1341→Research, Inc. shown below tensions the \n  1342→rails, which pulls the rail assembly straight. \n  1343→- \n  1344→TENSION STRAIGHTENS RAIL \n  1345→SPRING MAINTAINS TENSION \n  1346→REFERENCES \n  1347→1. “The Transformation of American Industry”, Productivity-Quality Systems, Inc. Module #1, page 3, 1986. \n  1348→2. “Radiation Plus Convection: The Total Solution”, Norman R. Cox, Research Inc., U.S.A., NEPCON West 1990. \n  1349→3. “Optimization of Solder Reflow with IR”, Norman R. Cox, Research, Inc., U.S.A., ISHM 1985. \n  1350→4. “Inert 1R Reflow: The Significance of Oxygen Concentration in the Atmosphere”, Colin Lea, National Physics \n  1351→Laboratory, Teddington, U.K., Surface Mount International, 199 I. \n  1352→Educational Publishing, Inc., page 3 I. \n  1353→5. Managerial and Engineering Economy, George A. Taylor, D. Van Nostrand Company, 0 1975 by Litton \n  1354→Section I11 - Research, Inc. Equipment Technology \n  1355→19 \n  1356→\n  1357→--- PAGE 22 ---\n  1358→PRODUCT MATRIX \n  1359→4470MP 4470XP \n  1360→36 \n  1361→96 \n  1362→N/A \n  1363→120 \n  1364→91 4 \n  1365→2438 \n  1366→3048 \n  1367→9-1 2 \n  1368→24-32 \n  1369→30-40 \n  1370→20-30 \n  1371→60-80 \n  1372→76-1 02 \n  1373→X \n  1374→X \n  1375→X \n  1376→X \n  1377→FEATURE \n  1378→I 4440 \n  1379→4470HP \n  1380→114 \n  1381→138 \n  1382→2895 \n  1383→3505 \n  1384→30-40 \n  1385→36-48 \n  1386→70-100 \n  1387→90-1 20 \n  1388→X \n  1389→X \n  1390→PROCESS AREA \n  1391→STDLENGTH IN \n  1392→OPTLENGTH IN \n  1393→4470HN \n  1394→148 \n  1395→NIA \n  1396→3759 \n  1397→36-50 \n  1398→90-130 \n  1399→X \n  1400→STDLENGTH MM \n  1401→OPTLENGTH MM \n  1402→4480XG \n  1403→96 \n  1404→N/A \n  1405→2438 \n  1406→24-32 \n  1407→60-80 \n  1408→X \n  1409→36 \n  1410→N/A \n  1411→91 4 \n  1412→STD \n  1413→N/A \n  1414→N/A \n  1415→X \n  1416→X \n  1417→90 \n  1418→47 \n  1419→2286 \n  1420→1194 \n  1421→PROCESS SPEED \n  1422→STD LENGTH IN/MIN \n  1423→OPT LENGTH IN/MIN \n  1424→STD \n  1425→OPT \n  1426→OPT \n  1427→X \n  1428→X \n  1429→168 \n  1430→45 \n  1431→4274 \n  1432→1143 \n  1433→STD LENGTH CM/MIN \n  1434→OPT LENGTH CM/MIN \n  1435→STD \n  1436→N/A \n  1437→N/A \n  1438→9-1 2 \n  1439→20-30 \n  1440→STD \n  1441→OPT \n  1442→OPT \n  1443→HEATER TECHNOLOGY \n  1444→FORCED CONVECTION \n  1445→IR/CONVECTION \n  1446→X \n  1447→X \n  1448→X \n  1449→X \n  1450→X \n  1451→PROCESS GAS \n  1452→AI R \n  1453→HIGH PURITY N, \n  1454→X \n  1455→CONVEYOR TYPE \n  1456→EDGE \n  1457→MESH \n  1458→DUAL (EDGE/MESH) \n  1459→N/A \n  1460→STD \n  1461→N/A \n  1462→CONTROLLER \n  1463→MICROPROCESSOR \n  1464→HOST INTERFACE \n  1465→X \n  1466→X \n  1467→DIMENSIONS \n  1468→LENGTH IN \n  1469→WIDTH \n  1470→IN \n  1471→LENGTH MM \n  1472→WIDTH \n  1473→MM \n  1474→90 \n  1475→50 \n  1476→2286 \n  1477→1258 \n  1478→VOLTAGES \n  1479→200V, 3PHASE, 50 HZ \n  1480→208V, 3PHASE, 60 HZ \n  1481→380V, 3PHASE, 50 HZ \n  1482→41 5V, 3PHASE, 50 HZ \n  1483→480V, 3PHASE, 60 HZ \n  1484→(AVAILABLE ON ALL MODELS) \n  1485→STD \n  1486→N/A \n  1487→N/A \n  1488→X \n  1489→X \n  1490→222 \n  1491→45 \n  1492→5639 \n  1493→1143 \n  1494→4470XN \n  1495→96 \n  1496→N/A \n  1497→2438 \n  1498→24-32 \n  1499→60-80 \n  1500→X \n  1501→X \n  1502→X \n  1503→STD \n  1504→OPT \n  1505→OPT \n  1506→X \n  1507→X \n  1508→168 \n  1509→45 \n  1510→4274 \n  1511→1143 \n  1512→1143 \n  1513→* Add 36 inches (914 mm) to overall machine \n  1514→length for Dual Conveyor Extension Option. \n  1515→20 \n  1516→\n  1517→--- PAGE 24 ---\n  1518→I \n  1519→1 \n  1520→1 \n  1521→! \n  1522→1 \n  1523→1 \n  1524→4000-I-01-A \n  1525→n \n  1526→Box 24064, Minneapolis, MN 55424 USA \n  1527→(612) 941 -3300 Telex 29-0502 Fax (612) 941 -3628\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]},"uuid":"85aba5a0-b58b-4dc2-a076-cf734103fec9","timestamp":"2026-02-07T05:28:27.351Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"5eae4a8a-a94e-42a5-888a-324539bc96cb"}
